# Next.js API Route Optimization

## Overview
Best practices for optimizing Next.js API routes and server-side data fetching.

## Parallel vs Sequential Fetching

### Problem: Sequential Fetching
```typescript
// Bad: Takes 3 seconds if each call takes 1 second
const user = await fetchUser();
const products = await fetchProducts();
const reviews = await fetchReviews();
```

### Solution: Parallel Fetching
```typescript
// Good: Takes 1 second (parallel execution)
const [user, products, reviews] = await Promise.all([
  fetchUser(),
  fetchProducts(),
  fetchReviews(),
]);
```

## API Route Optimization Patterns

### 1. Parallel Independent Requests
```typescript
export async function GET() {
  // Fetch independent data in parallel
  const [data1, data2, data3] = await Promise.all([
    fetchData1(),
    fetchData2(),
    fetchData3(),
  ]);

  return NextResponse.json({ data1, data2, data3 });
}
```

### 2. Sequential Dependent Requests
```typescript
export async function GET() {
  // First request
  const user = await fetchUser();

  // Parallel requests that depend on user
  const [profile, settings] = await Promise.all([
    fetchProfile(user.id),
    fetchSettings(user.id),
  ]);

  return NextResponse.json({ user, profile, settings });
}
```

### 3. Fire-and-Forget Operations
```typescript
export async function POST(request: Request) {
  const data = await request.json();

  // Don't await analytics logging
  logAnalytics(data).catch(console.error);

  // Return immediately
  return NextResponse.json({ success: true });
}
```

